t's standard fasting pathway", "choice_c": "Use a full-liquid diet until two hours before anesthesia", "choice_d": "Ignore dietary preparation when the patient has no nausea", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The Australian/New Zealand recommendations support continuation with a 24-hour clear-fluid diet followed by the stated standard six-hour fast. Apply that guidance in its local protocol context; do not combine it with unrelated fasting rules.", "source_ids": [3], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12420935/"], "learning_objective_ids": [2], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "What conclusion is supported by RESIDUAL's 42% versus 24% increased-RGC findings after at least a seven-day hold?", "choice_a": "A seven-day hold guarantees an empty stomach", "choice_b": "The study proved a statistically significant doubling of aspiration", "choice_c": "Retained contents can persist after a seven-day hold, but this underpowered comparison was statistically inconclusive", "choice_d": "All patients require a hold longer than 21 days", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "RESIDUAL measured gastric contents, not a proven difference in aspiration. Its confidence intervals included no difference and the study was underpowered.", "source_ids": [6], "source_urls": ["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42265610/"], "learning_objective_ids": [2, 5], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "Why should residual gastric content and pulmonary aspiration be reported separately?", "choice_a": "Retained contents are a surrogate risk marker; aspiration is a distinct clinical event", "choice_b": "Every patient with retained fluid will aspirate", "choice_c": "An increased-RGC odds ratio directly equals the absolute aspiration probability", "choice_d": "They are interchangeable names for the same outcome", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The meta-analysis found increased residual contents but did not demonstrate increased aspiration with low-certainty evidence. The outcomes cannot be treated as synonymous.", "source_ids": [4], "source_urls": ["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40230298/"], "learning_objective_ids": [1, 5], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "A patient has recurrent vomiting and cannot tolerate oral intake before an elective procedure. Under SPAQI, what is the appropriate next step?", "choice_a": "Proceed solely because one weekly dose was missed", "choice_b": "Permit a protein shake immediately before anesthesia", "choice_c": "Treat the symptoms as irrelevant if the patient denies reflux", "choice_d": "Refer for prescribing-team diet/medication modification and reassessment before elective anesthesia", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Severe nausea, vomiting, and inability to tolerate intake are significant symptoms in SPAQI's framework. They warrant modification and reassessment rather than reassurance from a fixed hold.", "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/"], "learning_objective_ids": [3, 4], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "Which statement best describes a reassuring gastric ultrasound assessment?", "choice_a": "One apparently empty antral image proves a completely empty stomach", "choice_b": "An inconclusive image should be